ation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nJ: 1, 7: 1, 4: 1\nValid Moves:\nsingle: ♠4\nsingle: ♣8\nsingle: ♠10\nsingle: ♥J\nsingle: ♣K\nsingle: ♦A\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation: This is a free play (no previous combination to beat), so I can choose any single card. The landlord has only 1 card left, meaning they are very close to winning. My teammate also has only 2 cards, so they are also near the end. Since the landlord is about to win, my priority must be to **prevent the landlord from winning** by either playing a low card to let my teammate take control or carefully managing rank flow.\n\nLooking at my hand: ♠4, ♣8, ♠10, ♥J, ♣K, ♦A — all singles. Playing high cards like ♦A or ♣K risks giving the landlord an easy pass and could block my teammate from playing if they have lower cards. Conversely, playing too low might force my teammate into a bad position.\n\nHowever, note that unknown cards include J (1), 7 (1), and 4 (1). Since I hold ♥J, and one J remains unknown, it\'s possible my teammate holds it or not. But importantly, **my teammate passed recently**, suggesting they may not want to play high unless forced.\n\nGiven the landlord has only 1 card, **I should play a low-to-mid card** to avoid wasting high power, while possibly allowing my teammate to continue if they have something just above it.\n\nThe lowest card I can play is ♠4. It’s safe because:\n- It doesn\'t waste high value.\n- If my teammate has a 5, 6, etc., they can potentially follow up.\n- The landlord already played ♠8 earlier and then passed later, suggesting they may not have strong mid-range now.\n\nAlternatively, playing ♣8 matches what the landlord played before, but since they passed afterward, it\'s unclear if they still have more 8s or not. Still, playing ♠4 is safer and keeps higher cards for later use.\n\nReason: Play ♠4 to maintain tempo, avoid wasting high cards, and give my teammate a chance to play slightly higher if they can, potentially setting up a chain where we block the landlord from going out.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4"]}'}
